    "https://careers.utrgv.edu/postings/52714": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice (Courts, Law, and Judicial Studies)/College of Liberal Arts",
      "description": "The University of Texas Rio Grande Valley Job Site | Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice (Courts, Law, and Judicial Studies)/College of Liberal Arts Skip to Main Content Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Office Location The University of Texas Rio Grande Valley 1201 W. University Drive Human Resources MASS Building 2 nd Floor Edinburg, TX 78539 Edinburg: (956)665-2451 Brownsville: (956) 882-8205 Office hours: Monday-Friday 8:00a.m. - 5:00p.m. Staff Recruitment: careers@utrgv.edu School of Medicine Recruitment: SOMCareers@utrgv.edu Graduate Assistantships: assistantships@utrgv.edu Faculty Recruitment: College of Engineering & Computer Science: cecs@utrgv.edu Robert C. Vackar College of Business & Entrepreneurship: cobe@utrgv.edu College of Sciences Office of the Dean: cos@utrgv.edu College of Education and P-16 Integration: cep@utrgv.edu College of Fine Arts: cofa@utrgv.edu College of Liberal Arts: liberalarts@utrgv.edu Website: https://careers.utrgv.edu Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice (Courts, Law, and Judicial Studies)/College of Liberal Arts Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Complete all sections on the application. If you are applying for the first time, please complete all biographical information including address, email and phone number. You may update this at any time by selecting to edit your profile in the application. Security sensitive; All UTRGV employees are required to have a criminal background check (CBC). CBCs will be conducted for all candidates invited for an on-campus interview. Posting Details Title Information Faculty Title Assistant Professor Job Class Code 10031 FLSA Exempt Position Information Posting Number FRGV2057 Working Title Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice (Courts, Law, and Judicial Studies)/College of Liberal Arts Number of Vacancies 1 Location Rio Grande Valley, Texas Division/Organization Provost - Academic Affairs Appointment Period for Non-Tenure Position Tenure Status Tenure Track FTE 1.0 Scope of Job We seek applicants who are dedicated to serving The University of Texas Rio Grande Valley’s student body as an Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year. The Department of Criminal Justice invites applications for one full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor of Criminal Justice (Courts, Law, and Judicial Studies). Areas of interest may include courts and judicial processes, criminal law and procedure, sentencing, prosecution and defense, plea bargaining, bail and pretrial justice, judicial decision-making, specialty and problem-solving courts, wrongful convictions, legal decision-making, court policy and reform, or related areas. The successful candidate will teach undergraduate and graduate courses within the candidate’s area of specialization and core Criminal Justice courses as departmental needs require, with opportunities to teach in both Traditional and Accelerated Online graduate formats. About UTRGV : UTRGV serves the Rio Grande Valley and beyond via an innovative and unique multicultural education dedicated to student access and success. By championing leading research, healthcare, and application of university discoveries, we support sustainable development, community engagement, and well-being. The University of Texas Rio Grande Valley ( UTRGV ) is committed to building and sustaining a highly qualified and talented faculty to pursue excellence in everything that we do, including teaching, research, and service. Drawing on our unique identity as the leading Hispanic-Serving Institution in a bilingual and bicultural environment, UTRGV will promote student access and success by building from strength in education and creating a vibrant campus experience. By 2027, we will accelerate transformation in the Rio Grande Valley and beyond by driving meaningful advancements in research and creative works, expanding access to high-quality and affordable healthcare, and fostering authentic community engagement and integration. UTRGV is a distributed campus, one university spanning four counties and multiple locations. Our purpose is to be a university for the entire Rio Grande Valley and beyond. Over 91% of the students enrolled at UTRGV identify as Hispanic, making UTRGV one of the top 3 universities with the highest percentage of Hispanic students. For additional information, please visit UTRGV website. Department/College Background The Department of Criminal Justice is part of the College of Liberal Arts at The University of Texas Rio Grande Valley and is one of the University’s largest departments. The Department offers the Bachelor of Science in Criminal Justice ( BSCJ ) and the Master of Science in Criminal Justice ( MSCJ ), including Traditional and Accelerated Online pathways. Fall 2025 BSCJ enrollment was approximately 2,245 students, and Fall 2025 MSCJ enrollment was approximately 90 students. Faculty are based primarily on the Edinburg and Brownsville campuses and support undergraduate and graduate education, research, professional engagement, and community partnerships. Minimum Qualifications A Ph.D. in Criminology, Criminal Justice, or a closely related field is required. Advanced ABD candidates will be considered, but all Ph.D. requirements must be completed by the start date. A Juris Doctorate alone does not meet the minimum qualifications. Candidates must demonstrate potential for scholarly productivity and the ability to work collaboratively in a multidisciplinary academic department. Discipline Specific Required Qualifications Candidates must demonstrate a teaching and research specialization in courts, judicial processes, criminal law and procedure, sentencing, or a closely related criminal justice area. Preferred Qualifications Preference will be given to candidates whose scholarship addresses contemporary issues involving courts and judicial processes, sentencing, prosecution and defense, criminal procedure, judicial decision-making, pretrial justice, wrongful convictions, specialty courts, or court policy and reform. Candidates with a demonstrated record of scholarly activity, experience teaching in classroom and online environments, potential for external funding and interdisciplinary collaboration, and the ability to mentor undergraduate and graduate research are especially encouraged to apply. A Ph.D. with a J.D. is desirable but not required; a J.D. alone does not satisfy the minimum qualification. License or Certification Required? No Salary Commensurate with Qualifications and Experience Desired Start Date 09/01/2027 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement It is the policy of The University of Texas Rio Grande Valley to promote and ensure equal employment opportunities for all individuals without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, age, religion, disability, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information or protected veteran status. In accordance with the requirements of Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, as amended, our University is committed to comply with all government requirements and ensures non-discrimination in its education programs and activities, including employment. We encourage women, minorities and differently abled persons to apply for employment positions of interest. Open Date 10/01/2026 Close Date 11/01/2026 Additional Information UTRGV is a distributed institution. As such and as assigned, the position may require presence at multiple locations throughout the Rio Grande Valley. Work is performed primarily in a general office environment. This position is security sensitive and thereby subject to the provisions of the Texas Education Code §51.215. The retirement plan for this position is Teacher Retirement System of Texas ( TRS ), subject to the position being at least 20 hours per week and at least 135 days in length. This position has the option to elect the Optional Retirement Program ( ORP ) instead of TRS , subject to the position being 40 hours per week and at least 135 days in length. Special Instructions to Applicants Complete all sections on the application. If you are applying for the first time, please complete all biographical information including address, email and phone number. You may update this at any time by selecting to edit your profile in the application. Security sensitive; All UTRGV employees are required to have a criminal background check ( CBC ). CBCs will be conducted for all candidates invited for an on-campus interview. Please Note Texas law requires faculty members whose primary language is not English to demonstrate proficiency in English as determined by a satisfactory paper-based test score of 500 (computer-based of 173 or internet-based of 61) on the Test of English as a Foreign Language ( TOEFL ) or a satisfactory test score of 6.0 on the International English Language Testing System ( IELTS ). Incomplete applications will not be considered. All positions are subject to budget approval. Quick Link https://careers.utrgv.edu/postings/52564 Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Curriculum Vitae Unofficial Transcripts List of 3 References Letter of Interest Optional Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Application Teaching Philosophy Writing / Publication Sample Reference Letter 1 Reference Letter 2 Reference Letter 3 Multi Media Certifications and Licenses Training Certificates Other Document Evidence of Teaching Effectiveness Unofficial Transcripts 2 Unofficial Transcripts 3 The University of Texas Rio Grande Valley UT System Employee Benefits UT System Fraud Reporting State of Texas Texas Homeland Security Texas Veterans Portal Where the money goes To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://careers.utrgv.edu/postings/52713": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor of Anthropology/College of Liberal Arts",
      "description": "The University of Texas Rio Grande Valley Job Site | Assistant Professor of Anthropology/College of Liberal Arts Skip to Main Content Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Office Location The University of Texas Rio Grande Valley 1201 W. University Drive Human Resources MASS Building 2 nd Floor Edinburg, TX 78539 Edinburg: (956)665-2451 Brownsville: (956) 882-8205 Office hours: Monday-Friday 8:00a.m. - 5:00p.m. Staff Recruitment: careers@utrgv.edu School of Medicine Recruitment: SOMCareers@utrgv.edu Graduate Assistantships: assistantships@utrgv.edu Faculty Recruitment: College of Engineering & Computer Science: cecs@utrgv.edu Robert C. Vackar College of Business & Entrepreneurship: cobe@utrgv.edu College of Sciences Office of the Dean: cos@utrgv.edu College of Education and P-16 Integration: cep@utrgv.edu College of Fine Arts: cofa@utrgv.edu College of Liberal Arts: liberalarts@utrgv.edu Website: https://careers.utrgv.edu Assistant Professor of Anthropology/College of Liberal Arts Below you will find the details for the position including any supplementary documentation and questions you should review before applying for the opening. To apply for the position, please click the Apply for this Job link/button. If you would like to bookmark this position for later review, click on the Bookmark link. If you would like to print a copy of this position for your records, click on the Print Preview link. Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Complete all sections on the application. If you are applying for the first time, please complete all biographical information including address, email and phone number. You may update this at any time by selecting to edit your profile in the application. Security sensitive; All UTRGV employees are required to have a criminal background check (CBC). CBCs will be conducted for all candidates invited for an on-campus interview. Posting Details Title Information Faculty Title Assistant Professor Job Class Code 10031 FLSA Exempt Position Information Posting Number FRGV2053 Working Title Assistant Professor of Anthropology/College of Liberal Arts Number of Vacancies 2 Location Rio Grande Valley, Texas Division/Organization Provost - Academic Affairs Appointment Period for Non-Tenure Position Tenure Status Tenure Track FTE 1.0 Scope of Job We seek applicants who are dedicated to serving The University of Texas Rio Grande Valley’s student body as Assistant Professors in the Department of Anthropology beginning in the 2027-2028 academic year. The Department of Anthropology seeks to fill two full-time, tenure-track Assistant Professor positions beginning September 1, 2027, through a single collaborative search. Applicants must identify the profile for which they wish to be considered. Profile 1: Biological Anthropology with a Forensic Anthropology Focus. Candidates should demonstrate research and teaching expertise in forensic anthropology and the capacity to support student learning in areas such as human identification, skeletal biology, search and recovery, or related analytical methods. Profile 2: Archaeology. Candidates should demonstrate research and teaching expertise in archaeology as well as the ability to contribute to field and laboratory training in ways that develop student learning opportunities. A geographic focus in the western or southwestern United States is preferred. The two appointments will be reviewed together, with attention to the distinctive qualifications of candidates in each profile and to the complementary contributions the two successful candidates can make to departmental programs, student opportunities, and collaborative research. Successful candidates will maintain active programs of research and scholarship; teach undergraduate and graduate courses; mentor students; and participate in departmental, university, professional, and community service. The department particularly values candidates who can create meaningful opportunities for students through field, laboratory, digital, archival, collections-based, or community-engaged work. UTRGV’s location in the Rio Grande Valley offers distinctive opportunities for anthropological research, teaching, and public engagement. The department welcomes candidates interested in developing ethical regional partnerships, connecting local questions to broader anthropological conversations, and creating applied learning and career pathways for students. In the letter of interest, applicants must identify the profile for which they wish to be considered: (1) Biological Anthropology with a Forensic Anthropology focus or (2) Archaeology. The letter should describe the applicant’s research trajectory, teaching contributions, plans for involving students in research or applied learning, and interest in the opportunities available through UTRGV and the Rio Grande Valley. About UTRGV : UTRGV serves the Rio Grande Valley and beyond via an innovative and unique multicultural education dedicated to student access and success. By championing leading research, healthcare, and application of university discoveries, we support sustainable development, community engagement, and well-being. The University of Texas Rio Grande Valley ( UTRGV ) is committed to building and sustaining a highly qualified and talented faculty to pursue excellence in everything that we do, including teaching, research, and service. Drawing on our unique identity as the leading Hispanic-Serving Institution in a bilingual and bicultural environment, UTRGV will promote student access and success by building from strength in education and creating a vibrant campus experience. By 2027, we will accelerate transformation in the Rio Grande Valley and beyond by driving meaningful advancements in research and creative works, expanding access to high-quality and affordable healthcare, and fostering authentic community engagement and integration. UTRGV is a distributed campus, one university spanning four counties and multiple locations. Our purpose is to be a university for the entire Rio Grande Valley and beyond. Over 91% of the students enrolled at UTRGV identify as Hispanic, making UTRGV one of the top 3 universities with the highest percentage of Hispanic students. For additional information, please visit UTRGV website . Department and College Background The UTRGV Department of Anthropology offers a Bachelor of Arts in Anthropology with concentrations in Archaeology and Global Health and Migration; minors in Anthropology, Archaeology, Forensic Anthropology, and Global Health and Migration; and an anthropology concentration in the Master of Arts in Interdisciplinary Studies. The department emphasizes applied, publicly engaged anthropology and provides students with opportunities for experiential learning. Departmental and affiliated resources include the Forensic Anthropology Research Laboratory ( FAR .Lab), the Anthropology Digital Visualization Lab ( ADVL ), the Community Historical Archaeology Project with Schools ( CHAPS ), and the Border Studies Archive ( BSA ). Faculty may also develop appropriate collaborations across the College of Liberal Arts and with units in the biological sciences, health sciences, criminal justice, public affairs, and other fields. Minimum Qualifications A Ph.D. in Anthropology or a closely related field from an accredited institution is required no later than August 31, 2027. Applicants must demonstrate evidence of, or strong potential for, excellence in teaching; an active and sustainable program of research and scholarship; and effective service appropriate to a tenure-track appointment. Discipline Specific Required Qualifications Candidates must have completed at least 18 graduate credit hours in anthropology. Applicants must demonstrate doctoral preparation and a research agenda appropriate to the profile for which they are applying, as well as the ability to teach anthropology courses at the undergraduate level and contribute, as appropriate, to graduate instruction and student mentoring. Candidates whose work involves human remains, animal remains, human participants, community knowledge, collections, or sensitive data must demonstrate knowledge of applicable professional, legal, and ethical standards. Preferred Qualifications Preference will be given to candidates whose records demonstrate the potential to involve students in research, develop applied or experiential learning opportunities, pursue appropriate external support and collaborative projects, contribute to upper-division and graduate instruction, and build ethical and mutually beneficial professional or community partnerships. License or Certification Required? No Salary Commensurate with Qualifications and Experience Desired Start Date 09/01/2027 Posting Detail Information EEO Statement It is the policy of The University of Texas Rio Grande Valley to promote and ensure equal employment opportunities for all individuals without regard to race, color, national origin, sex, age, religion, disability, sexual orientation, gender identity or expression, genetic information or protected veteran status. In accordance with the requirements of Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, the Title IX of the Education Amendments of 1972, Section 504 of the Rehabilitation Act of 1973, and the Americans with Disabilities Act of 1990, as amended, our University is committed to comply with all government requirements and ensures non-discrimination in its education programs and activities, including employment. We encourage women, minorities and differently abled persons to apply for employment positions of interest. Open Date 10/01/2026 Close Date 11/01/2026 Additional Information UTRGV is a distributed institution. As such and as assigned, the position may require presence at multiple locations throughout the Rio Grande Valley. Work is performed primarily in a general office environment. This position is security sensitive and thereby subject to the provisions of the Texas Education Code §51.215. The retirement plan for this position is Teacher Retirement System of Texas ( TRS ), subject to the position being at least 20 hours per week and at least 135 days in length. This position has the option to elect the Optional Retirement Program ( ORP ) instead of TRS , subject to the position being 40 hours per week and at least 135 days in length. Special Instructions to Applicants Complete all sections on the application. If you are applying for the first time, please complete all biographical information including address, email and phone number. You may update this at any time by selecting to edit your profile in the application. Security sensitive; All UTRGV employees are required to have a criminal background check ( CBC ). CBCs will be conducted for all candidates invited for an on-campus interview. Please Note Texas law requires faculty members whose primary language is not English to demonstrate proficiency in English as determined by a satisfactory paper-based test score of 500 (computer-based of 173 or internet-based of 61) on the Test of English as a Foreign Language ( TOEFL ) or a satisfactory test score of 6.0 on the International English Language Testing System ( IELTS ). Incomplete applications will not be considered. All positions are subject to budget approval. Quick Link https://careers.utrgv.edu/postings/52552 Posting Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Writing / Publication Sample Curriculum Vitae Unofficial Transcripts List of 3 References Letter of Interest Optional Documents Teaching Philosophy Certifications and Licenses The University of Texas Rio Grande Valley UT System Employee Benefits UT System Fraud Reporting State of Texas Texas Homeland Security Texas Veterans Portal Where the money goes To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
